Only the component of the angular momentum along the axis of rotation is considered, so
$
  L = bold(L) dot bold(hat(n)) = omega sum_i m_i (bold(hat(n)) times bold(x_i)) dot (bold(hat(n)) times bold(x_i)) = omega I.
$
If the torque is also along the axis of rotation, $bold(G) = G bold(hat(n))$, then dotting $bold(dot(L)) = bold(G)$ with $bold(hat(n))$ gives
$
  G = I dot(omega).
$
Hence $G$ acts like a _rotational force_, causing change in the angular velocity.
